<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/kobiler-icin-yapay-zeka.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">Your competitor now prepares quotes in five minutes instead of half an hour, replies to customer messages even at midnight and gets the monthly report with a single click. Behind it isn’t a big budget, but <b>AI put in the right place</b>.</p>
      <p>To most SME owners, AI looks either like an overhyped fad or an expensive technology that “isn’t for us”. Yet today <b>AI for SMEs</b> is a very concrete toolset that starts with a few monthly subscriptions and, when set up properly, saves hours every week.</p>
      <p>In this article you’ll find current data on where AI adoption stands in Türkiye, 10 use cases small and medium-sized businesses can implement right away, the legal and practical risks to watch out for, and a step-by-step roadmap for a business starting from scratch.</p>
      <h2 id="turkiye-verileri" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">AI adoption by businesses in Türkiye: 2026 data</h2>
      <p>The Artificial Intelligence Statistics published by TÜİK (the Turkish Statistical Institute) in October 2026 show the picture is changing fast. The AI adoption rate among enterprises with 10 or more employees rose from 2.7 percent in 2021 to 7.5 percent in 2025 and 14 percent in 2026. In other words, it almost doubled in a single year.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Enterprise size</th><th>AI adoption rate (2026)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>10–49 employees</td><td>%12,8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>50–249 employees</td><td>%17,0</td></tr>
          <tr><td>250 or more employees</td><td>%37,1</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The gap is striking: adoption in large companies is almost three times that of small businesses. Among individuals, the share using generative AI rose from 19.2 percent to 37.6 percent over the same period; in other words, many of your employees already use these tools in their private lives.</p>
      <p>Of the enterprises that have considered using AI but not yet started, 72.3 percent cite <b>lack of expertise</b> as the biggest obstacle, followed by legal uncertainty and data privacy concerns. In short, the problem isn’t access to technology but knowing where to start.</p>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>The biggest obstacle to AI for SMEs isn’t technology, but a lack of knowledge and a roadmap.</blockquote>
      <h2 id="kullanim-alanlari" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">10 practical AI use cases for SMEs</h2>
      <p>What these areas have in common is that you can start without a large software project, often with off-the-shelf tools or small integrations:</p>
      <h3>Customers and sales</h3>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>1. Customer service assistant:</b> A chat assistant that answers frequent questions coming in via your website and WhatsApp (prices, delivery times, opening hours, order status) and hands complex requests over to a person.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>2. Quote and email drafts:</b> Preparing quote texts, follow-up emails and contract drafts in minutes with templates fed by your product catalogue and past quotes.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>3. Marketing content:</b> Product descriptions, social media posts, blog drafts and ad copy. According to TÜİK’s 2025 bulletin, 46.5 percent of enterprises using AI use it in marketing and sales, the most common purpose on the list.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>4. Analysing customer reviews:</b> Grouping complaints in Google reviews, surveys and support requests by topic, and spotting recurring problems early.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Operations and finance</h3>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>5. Document reading and data entry:</b> Automatically extracting information from documents such as invoices, delivery notes and order forms and transferring it into your system. It also reduces manual data entry errors.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>6. Accounting and pre-checks:</b> In the same bulletin, 33.7 percent of enterprises using AI use it in accounting, controlling or financial management. Categorising expenses, flagging unusual amounts, cash flow summaries. The final word should always rest with your accountant.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>7. Inventory and demand forecasting:</b> Forecasting seasonal demand from past sales data to reduce the risk of overstocking or running out of stock.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>8. Reporting and conversational analysis:</b> Asking questions like “Which product was returned most last month?” in natural language and getting tables and charts.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Team and knowledge management</h3>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>9. Internal knowledge assistant:</b> An assistant that works on procedures, product documentation and FAQs; it shortens onboarding time for new employees.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>10. Meeting and correspondence summaries:</b> Turning meeting notes into task lists, summarising long email threads, translating texts into other languages.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Where to start?</b> Pick just one area from the list: the most repetitive, the easiest to measure and the least harmful if something goes wrong. Internal processes that don’t directly touch customers are usually the safest start.</div></div>
      <h2 id="hazir-mi-ozel-mi" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">Off-the-shelf AI tools or a custom integration?</h2>
      <p>There are three levels at which SMEs can work with AI. For most businesses the right path is to start at level one and move up as needed:</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Example</th><th>When is it suitable?</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Off-the-shelf tools</td><td>General-purpose chat assistants, AI features built into office suites</td><td>Text, summaries, translation, ideation; to get started right away</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Features added to existing software</td><td>AI modules in CRM, e-commerce and accounting software</td><td>If the data is already in a system you use</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. Custom integration</td><td>An assistant connected to your own data, document reading, a chatbot linked to your order system</td><td>If the process is unique to you and volume is high</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>The third level requires AI to connect securely to your company data and your order or inventory system. At this point the work actually becomes a <a href="ozel-yazilim-gelistirme">custom software development</a> project: issues such as authorisation, logging and handing over to a human when something goes wrong are at least as important as the model itself.</p>
      <h2 id="riskler" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">Risks of using AI: KVKK, accuracy and security</h2>
      <p>AI is a powerful assistant, but it doesn’t take over responsibility. Know these risks before you start:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Personal data:</b> Pasting data such as customer names, phone numbers, health information or employee payroll into general-purpose tools carries serious risk under KVKK. The Personal Data Protection Authority published its “Guide on Generative AI and the Protection of Personal Data” on this subject in November 2025.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Transferring data abroad:</b> Most cloud-based AI services process data on servers abroad. Article 9 of Law No. 6698 was amended in 2024, introducing new mechanisms such as standard contracts for cross-border transfers; check which tool processes data under which conditions.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Wrong but confident answers:</b> AI models can sometimes produce false information in a convincing way. Critical content such as prices, regulations and technical specifications must always be checked by a human.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Trade secrets:</b> Quote prices, cost tables and customer lists should not be shared except on business plans that commit not to use data for model training.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Uncontrolled use:</b> Your employees may already be using these tools. Rather than banning them, it is more effective to write a short company policy explaining which tool can be used with which data for which task.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>Note:</b> This section is for general information only and is not legal advice. For AI projects that process personal data, we recommend getting an opinion from a KVKK expert.</div></div>
      <h2 id="yol-haritasi" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">How to get started with AI: a 5-step roadmap</h2>
      <p>Moving forward with small, measurable experiments instead of a big transformation project keeps both risk and budget under control:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 1 — Map your time:</b> List the tasks your team repeats every week and how long they take. The best candidates for AI are frequent, text- or data-heavy tasks.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 2 — Choose a single pilot:</b> For example, “preparing a first draft reply to customer emails”. Write down the success criteria up front: response time, hours spent, error rate.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 3 — Set the rules:</b> Which data can be shared, and which outputs can’t go out without human approval? Write a short usage policy.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 4 — Try it for 4–6 weeks and measure:</b> Compare the before and after figures during the pilot. Collect the team’s feedback; you’ll often find that a process change, not a tool, is needed.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 5 — Scale up or drop it:</b> If the results are good, make it permanent and integrate it with your existing systems if needed. If the results are weak, move on to the next area on the list.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Don’t decide without measuring</h3>
      <p>Evaluate the pilot’s success with numbers, not gut feeling. For example, if the average first response time to customer emails has dropped from four hours to under one hour and the team saves a few hours a week, comparing the monthly value of that with the tool’s subscription fee makes the decision easier. Likewise, record the share of outputs that were wrong or needed correcting; if it is high, the problem is usually not the tool but the instructions given or the quality of the source data.</p>
      <p>Involving the team is just as important as measuring. The people who will use the tool every day know best where it really saves time and where it adds work. A short training session, a shared document of good example prompts and a ten-minute weekly review noticeably speed up adoption.</p>
      <p>The prerequisite for getting value from AI is well-organised data. If you don’t have a system that records orders, customers and stock consistently, it is often wiser to make your first investment there.</p>
      <h2 id="sektor-ornekleri" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">AI use cases by industry</h2>
      <p>The same technology solves different problems in different industries. A few typical examples:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>E-commerce:</b> Generating product descriptions in bulk, analysing return reasons, automatic replies to customers asking about order status. If you are new to e-commerce, <a href="e-ticaret-sitesi-kurma-rehberi">our guide to setting up an e-commerce site</a> covers the basic steps.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Restaurants and cafés:</b> Seeing from sales data which product sells more on which day and at what time, planning ingredient orders accordingly, summarising customer reviews.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Services and consulting:</b> Meeting summaries, quote and report drafts, an assistant working on an internal knowledge base.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Manufacturing and trade:</b> Scanning technical documents and specifications, comparing supplier quotes, demand forecasting.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Residential and building management:</b> Classifying resident requests by topic, automatic answers to frequent questions, drafting announcements.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>In all of these examples, AI creates value as a layer added on top of a digital infrastructure that already works. You can see examples of the products we have developed in areas such as restaurants and residential management on our <a href="projelerimiz">projects</a> page.</p>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// CHECKLIST BEFORE STARTING WITH AI</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have repetitive tasks and the time they take been listed?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Has a single process and success criterion been chosen for the pilot?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is it written down which data must not be shared?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have the data processing and storage terms of the tool been read?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is there a human approval step for critical outputs?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Has a short usage policy been prepared for employees?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>In which areas do SMEs use AI most?</summary><p>According to TÜİK’s 2025 Artificial Intelligence Statistics, the most common purpose among enterprises using AI is marketing and sales at 46.5 percent; production and service processes, research and development and business process management follow at around 40 percent, and accounting and financial management at 33.7 percent. In practice, customer service, document processing and reporting are also among the areas where SMEs often start.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Is using AI expensive?</summary><p>Not to get started. Business plans for general-purpose AI tools are offered as monthly per-user subscriptions. Costs rise with custom integrations connected to your company data and with high-volume use.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Will AI replace employees?</summary><p>At SME scale, the most common effect is that employees are freed from repetitive tasks and spend more time on customers and decision-making. Control and responsibility should always remain with people.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Is entering customer data into AI tools a violation of KVKK?</summary><p>Transferring personal data to these tools without a legal basis, proper notice and compliance with cross-border transfer requirements is risky under KVKK. We recommend reviewing the Personal Data Protection Authority’s generative AI guide and getting expert advice if needed.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>How much data do I need to start with AI?</summary><p>Tasks such as generating text, summarising and translating require no company data. For uses such as forecasting, analysis and internal assistants, however, well-organised, consistently recorded data directly determines the quality of the results.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>AI for SMEs starts not with big budgets and complex projects, but with a single well-chosen process. As adoption among businesses in Türkiye doubled in a year, small businesses that start early are gaining a real edge in speed and customer experience. What matters is keeping your data organised, moving forward with awareness of the risks and measuring the results.</p>
      <p>If you would like to discuss how to connect AI to your existing website, ordering or management system, <a href="iletisim">write to us</a> and let’s decide together on the right first step for your business.</p>
